== What the retained data show
The two p=4099 Prym matrices contain 97/67 witnesses but 13/9 distinct affine
equations, rank 13/15 and 9/15, and nullity 2/6. No individual log column is
identified. Each matrix identifies one two-column weighted combination chosen
after observing it. Those combinations are constructed controls, not natural
unknown-scalar targets. Both fixtures contain the public x=0 generator as
their first atom, so testing zero completion requires no larger base.

The retained combined F4 fixture has existing full-base rank 5. Eliminating
its outside columns adds 4 independent ranks to reach 9. The separate
one-outside/two-outside fixtures add 4/1, and the signed triangle adds 0.
These fixtures were constructed. No natural collision or throughput estimate
can be inferred. All input hashes and equation certificates are retained.

== First experimental wave
*B1, isolated zero completion:* same two curves, 16 atoms, 64-source order and
cubic law; fresh historical and completed-zero arms. The full paired workload
is 9,175,040 scheduled branches, derived before execution. Score distinct
equations and old-kernel syndromes before rank, retaining every failure.

*C1, Dickson depth 4:* exact existing F1 inputs and all multiplicity strata,
maximum operator dimension 560, complete signed/state oracle, nonseparable
and corrupted-certificate controls. Record costs by stage; small degree and
a completed toy case are not complexity evidence.

*A1, useful partial rows:* consume complete naturally generated signed partial
rows and compare cancellation with the dense oracle on identical rows. Charge
outside vertices, zero combinations, duplicates and elimination. No random-graph
collision assumption is justified automatically.

*D1/D2, natural targets:* freeze public hash-derived targets independently of
the matrix; first test row identifiability, then combinations in an augmented
system retaining the target coefficient. Full base rank is not required.
Eight proposed targets have not been generated. Existing full-rank E-stage
gates remain unchanged; this is a separate target-specific study.

